import json,re
from pathlib import Path
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]; out=root/'site/dist'
titles=['Introduction','Some Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics','National Income and Related Aggregates','Measurement of National Income','Money','Banking','Aggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts','Short Run Equilibrium Output','Problem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand','Government Budget and the Economy','Foreign Exchange Rate','Balance of Payments']
chapters=[dict(id=str(i+1),title=t,intro='',lessons=[],pages=[],boxes=[]) for i,t in enumerate(titles)]
for f in sorted((root/'extracted').glob('IMG*.json')):
 d=json.loads(f.read_text()); folder=d['file'].split('/')[0]; num=(1 if f.stem<'IMG_0503' else 2) if folder=='macro - 1 + 2' else int(folder.split()[-1]); c=chapters[num-1]
 text='\n'.join(l['text'] for l in d['lines']);page={'id':f.stem,'image':'pages/'+f.stem+'.jpg','text':text,'file':d['file']};c['pages'].append(page)
 # The image is the authoritative version; index every boxed/extension heading without silently dropping the surrounding page.
 lines=text.splitlines()
 for j,line in enumerate(lines):
  if re.search(r'CRITICAL TERMS|CRITICAL QUESTIONS|ABILITY ZONE|DID YOU KNOW|NOTE CAREFULLY|Power Points',line,re.I):
   if j<55 and re.search('Power Points',line) and 'LAYOUT' in text:continue
   end=next((k for k in range(j+1,len(lines)) if re.match(r'^\d{1,2}\.\d+\s+[A-Z]',lines[k]) or re.search(r'CRITICAL TERMS|CRITICAL QUESTIONS|ABILITY ZONE|Power Points',lines[k],re.I)),len(lines))
   c['boxes'].append({'title':re.sub(r'^[!•|I\s]+','',line),'page':f.stem,'text':'\n'.join(lines[j+1:end]),'image':page['image']})
def L(n,title,body,formula='',source='',kind='Concept'):
 chapters[n-1]['lessons'].append(dict(title=title,body=body,formula=formula,source=source,kind=kind))
L(1,'Meaning, origin and principal concerns','Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole. The term Macro comes from Makros, meaning large. Keynes’ The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936) established macroeconomics as a separate branch. Its principal concerns are employment, inflation, growth of output and depression. Keynes stressed government intervention when inadequate demand reduces output and employment.',source='IMG_0499')
L(1,'Microeconomics versus macroeconomics','Microeconomics studies scarcity and choice at the level of an individual, household, firm or industry; allocation of resources is its central issue. Macroeconomics studies these problems for the economy as a whole; determination of overall output and employment is its central issue. Micro uses partial equilibrium analysis, studying one market while holding other markets constant. Macro uses general equilibrium analysis, examining simultaneous equilibrium across markets. An industry is an aggregate, but studying one industry is still microeconomics.',source='IMG_0500')
L(1,'Micro–macro paradox and interdependence','What is logical at the micro level may not be logical at the macro level. Saving may improve one person’s future prosperity; if everyone saves more by spending less, aggregate demand, investment, output and employment may fall. Macro affects micro when an RBI interest-rate change affects one producer’s borrowing. Micro affects macro when individual increases in spending together raise aggregate demand.',source='IMG_0500',kind='Purple-box concept')
L(1,'Scope and significance','Macroeconomics studies national income, employment, general prices, growth, money, banking, government budgets and international transactions. It helps explain an economy’s functioning and formulate policies for growth with stability. Keep the level of analysis clear: the price of one product is micro; the general price level is macro.',source='IMG_0501')
L(2,'Final goods, intermediate goods and end use','Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users. They lead to final consumption expenditure or investment expenditure. Intermediate goods are purchased by one firm from another for resale or for use as raw material in producing other goods. Classification depends on end use, not the physical nature of the good: milk bought by a household is final; milk bought by an ice-cream producer is intermediate. Unsold additions to inventory count as investment.',source='IMG_0503')
L(2,'Consumption goods versus capital goods','Consumption goods directly satisfy human wants. They include durable goods, non-durable goods, semi-durable goods and services. Capital goods are fixed assets of producers, repeatedly used in production: durable-use producer goods such as plant and machinery. A household car is a durable consumption good; a taxi used to produce transport services is a capital good. Both are final goods. A capital good is not intermediate merely because it helps produce something.',source='IMG_0504')
L(2,'Consumption expenditure and investment','Consumption expenditure includes expenditure by households, government and non-profit private institutions. Investment is a process of adding to the stock of capital. Fixed investment adds to fixed assets; inventory investment changes stocks of raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods. New residential construction is investment. Buying existing shares transfers financial ownership; it does not itself create new capital goods. Inventory investment can be negative when closing stock is less than opening stock.','Inventory investment = Closing stock − Opening stock\nGross investment = Fixed investment + Inventory investment',source='IMG_0507')
L(2,'Gross investment, net investment and depreciation','Net investment refers to the increase in the stock of capital during an accounting year. Gross investment includes replacement of worn-out capital. Depreciation, also called consumption of fixed capital, refers to loss of value of fixed assets in use through normal wear and tear, normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence. Unexpected destruction is a capital loss rather than normal depreciation. A depreciation reserve fund is kept to cope with depreciation losses.','Net investment = Gross investment − Depreciation\nDepreciation per year = (Capital value − Scrap value) ÷ Estimated life',source='IMG_0508')
L(2,'Stocks and flows','Stocks are quantities measured at a point of time; flows are quantities measured over a specified period of time. Wealth, money supply, capital and bank balances are stocks. Income, consumption, investment and saving are flows. Capital is a stock; investment is the flow adding to it. “Money supply on 31 March” is a stock even though money changes hands repeatedly.',source='IMG_0509')
L(2,'Four sectors and circular flow of income','The household sector supplies factor services and consumes goods. The producing sector produces goods and services. Government acts as a welfare agency and producer; the external sector links the domestic economy with the rest of the world. Real flows are flows of goods and factor services; money flows are payments and receipts in the opposite direction. Circular flow of income refers to the circularity of production, income and expenditure across sectors. In a simple two-sector model, production generates income and income generates expenditure.','Production → Income generation → Expenditure → Production\nValue of output = Factor income = Final expenditure',source='IMG_0512')
L(3,'National income, factor incomes and transfer incomes','National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year. Factor incomes are rewards for factor services: compensation of employees, rent, interest and profit. Transfer incomes such as gifts, charity and old-age pensions are unilateral receipts without a current productive service in return; they are excluded. An employer’s retirement pension earned through service differs from an old-age welfare pension.',source='IMG_0515')
L(3,'Normal residents and domestic territory','A normal resident ordinarily resides in a country and has a centre of economic interest there. Citizenship alone does not determine residence. Tourists and visitors for medical treatment do not become residents merely through travel. Diplomats remain residents of their home countries; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic territory is economic territory: Indian embassies abroad belong to India’s domestic territory; foreign embassies in India do not. Check the Ability Zone for ships, aircraft, fishing vessels and other special cases.',source='IMG_0516')
L(3,'Domestic income versus national income','Domestic income is the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic territory, whether earned by residents or non-residents. National income belongs to normal residents, wherever earned. Net factor income from abroad is factor income received from the rest of the world minus factor income paid to the rest of the world. Remittances that are gifts are transfers, not NFIA.','NNP at FC = NDP at FC + NFIA\nNFIA = Factor income from abroad − Factor income to abroad',source='IMG_0517')
L(3,'The three conversion bridges','Use three independent adjustments. Gross to net: subtract depreciation. Domestic to national: add NFIA. Market price to factor cost: subtract net indirect taxes, which equal indirect taxes minus subsidies. Reverse the sign when travelling backwards. A negative NFIA reduces national income; negative NIT means subsidies exceed indirect taxes. Do not deduct indirect taxes again if NIT is already supplied.','NNPFC = GDPMP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA\nGDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA',source='IMG_0519')
L(3,'Eight related aggregates','GDPMP, GDPFC, NDPMP and NDPFC are domestic measures; GNPMP, GNPFC, NNPMP and NNPFC are national measures. Gross measures include consumption of fixed capital; net measures exclude it. Market-price measures include net indirect taxes; factor-cost measures exclude them. National income means NNPFC; domestic income means NDPFC. Identify all three labels before substituting a number.',source='IMG_0520')
L(3,'Nominal GDP, real GDP and GDP deflator','Nominal GDP is valued at current-year prices; real GDP is valued at base-year prices. Real GDP helps isolate changes in physical production from changes in prices. Nominal GDP may rise with unchanged output simply because prices rise. The GDP deflator is the ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP multiplied by 100. Use the price index as a percentage index, not as a decimal, in the formula below.','Nominal GDP = Σ(Current price × Current quantity)\nReal GDP = Σ(Base-year price × Current quantity)\nGDP deflator = Nominal GDP ÷ Real GDP × 100\nReal GDP = Nominal GDP ÷ GDP deflator × 100',source='IMG_0523')
L(3,'GDP and welfare: why output is an incomplete measure','A rise in real GDP can expand available goods and services, but it does not ensure an equal rise in welfare. The textbook highlights distribution of income, composition of GDP, non-monetary transactions and externalities. Unequal distribution can leave most people without gains; more military goods may not improve consumption; unpaid household work is omitted; pollution imposes an unpriced cost. Positive externalities are benefits not paid for; negative externalities are costs not compensated.',source='IMG_0525')
L(4,'Value added method: measure production once','Value added is value of output minus intermediate consumption. Value of output equals sales plus change in stock, with own-account production added where applicable. Sum GVA at market price across producing units to obtain GDPMP. Deduct depreciation and NIT, then add NFIA for national income. Alternatively, sum sectoral NVAFC and add NFIA.','Value of output = Sales + Closing stock − Opening stock\nGVA MP = Value of output − Intermediate consumption\nNNPFC = ΣGVA MP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA',source='IMG_0528')
L(4,'Double counting and value-added precautions','Double counting means counting the value of the same output more than once. Avoid it by counting final goods only or summing value added at each stage. If wheat sells for 100, flour for 160 and bread for 250, total value added is 100 + 60 + 90 = 250, not 510. Include own-account construction and imputed rent of owner-occupied houses. Exclude resale value of second-hand goods, but include the current broker’s service. Do not count intermediate goods separately from the final product.',source='IMG_0530')
L(4,'Income method: compensation, surplus and mixed income','Income method measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes generated within domestic territory. Compensation of employees includes wages and salaries in cash and kind and employers’ social-security contributions. Operating surplus comprises rent and royalty, interest and profit. Profit includes corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings; these are components, so do not add them again if total profit is given. Mixed income of the self-employed combines labour and property income that cannot be separated.','NDPFC = Compensation of employees + Operating surplus + Mixed income\nOperating surplus = Rent and royalty + Interest + Profit\nNational income = NDPFC + NFIA',source='IMG_0534')
L(4,'Income-method precautions','Include only incomes earned from current productive services. Exclude transfer receipts, capital gains and windfall gains. Exclude interest on consumption loans and interest on public debt when it represents a transfer rather than payment for a productive service, following the textbook treatment. Include imputed rent and production for self-consumption where measured. Do not count sale proceeds of shares or second-hand goods as factor income; the current commission or brokerage is a service income.',source='IMG_0535')
L(4,'Expenditure method and its precautions','Expenditure method measures final expenditure on domestically produced output. Add private final consumption expenditure, government final consumption expenditure, gross domestic capital formation and net exports. Gross domestic capital formation includes fixed capital formation and change in stocks. Exclude financial-asset purchases, transfers and second-hand purchases; include current brokerage. Subtract imports because imported content may already be included in consumption or investment. Do not add change in stock twice if gross domestic capital formation already includes it.','GDPMP = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M)\nNNPFC = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M) − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA',source='IMG_0538')
L(4,'Reconciliation and treatment of tricky items','The three methods measure the same current production from different sides: value added creates factor income, and income finances final expenditure. In an exam, classify each item before calculating. Government services are counted even when provided free; employer provident-fund contributions belong in compensation. Scholarships and welfare pensions are transfers. Foreign factor earnings require a residence/territory check; the purchase of a newly produced house is investment. Read the supplied transaction table for item-by-item domestic and national treatment.',source='IMG_0542')
L(5,'Money, barter and the evolution of money','Money is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange. Barter exchanges goods directly for goods. Its difficulties include lack of double coincidence of wants, lack of a common measure of value, difficulty storing wealth and difficulty making deferred payments. Money separates selling from buying: the seller need not want the precise good the buyer offers. Evolution moves through commodity money, metallic money, paper money and bank/credit money.',source='IMG_0545')
L(5,'Fiat, fiduciary and legal-tender money','Fiat money is money issued by authority of the government. Fiduciary money is accepted on trust between payer and payee. Legal tender must be accepted in discharge of debts within the applicable legal conditions; a cheque depends on acceptance and is not legal tender. Full-bodied money has face value equal to intrinsic commodity value; credit money has face value greater than intrinsic value. Follow the textbook distinctions, not the everyday use of the word “cash”.',source='IMG_0547')
L(5,'Primary and secondary functions of money','The primary functions are medium of exchange and measure of value (unit of account). Secondary functions are standard of deferred payments, store of value and transfer of value. Buying a book illustrates medium of exchange; quoting its price illustrates measure of value; paying a loan next year illustrates deferred payment; keeping purchasing power for later illustrates store of value. Inflation reduces the purchasing power stored in money.',source='IMG_0548')
L(5,'Money supply and its components','Money supply is the stock of money held by the public at a particular point of time. Interbank deposits and money held by the government/banking system are excluded from money held by the public. M1 comprises currency with the public, demand deposits with commercial banks and other deposits with the RBI. Demand deposits are withdrawable on demand. A cheque transfers a deposit; it is not an additional independent stock of money.','M1 = Currency with public + Demand deposits with banks + Other deposits with RBI',source='IMG_0549')
L(5,'Ability Zone: broader measures and liquidity','The textbook also gives M2, M3 and M4 for reference. M2 adds post-office savings-bank deposits to M1. M3 adds net time deposits with commercial banks to M1 and is called aggregate monetary resources. M4 adds total post-office deposits, excluding National Savings Certificates, to M3. Liquidity means convertibility into cash: money is the most liquid asset. The textbook’s monetary-system notes discuss paper currency, limited/unlimited legal tender and the minimum reserve system; the original page is available to read exactly.',source='IMG_0551',kind='Ability Zone')
L(6,'Commercial banks and their functions','Commercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit. Deposits include current, savings and fixed deposits. Primary functions are accepting deposits and lending; other functions include agency services and general-utility services. Banks mobilise saving and create demand deposits through lending. A central bank is the apex institution regulating the monetary and financial system; its objective is growth with stability rather than commercial profit.',source='IMG_0552')
L(6,'Credit creation: follow every round','Suppose an initial cash deposit of 1,000 enters the banking system and the reserve ratio is 20%. The first bank keeps 200 and lends 800. If 800 is redeposited, the next bank keeps 160 and lends 640. Repeated lending and redepositing expand total deposits to 5,000. These results assume no cash leakage, no excess reserves and loans redeposited in the banking system. Total deposits include the initial deposit; additional credit excludes it.','Deposit multiplier = 1 ÷ Reserve ratio\nTotal deposits = Initial deposit ÷ Reserve ratio\nAdditional credit = Total deposits − Initial deposit',source='IMG_0554')
L(6,'Central bank: seven functions','The textbook lists issuer of currency, banker to the government, bankers’ bank and supervisor, lender of last resort, custodian of foreign exchange, clearing-house function and control of credit. As government banker it holds accounts, receives money, makes payments and manages borrowing. As bankers’ bank it holds bank reserves and supports banks. Clearing settles interbank claims. Lender of last resort provides emergency assistance when normal sources cannot meet a bank’s needs.',source='IMG_0555')
L(6,'Quantitative instruments: rates and open market operations','Bank rate, repo rate and reverse repo rate influence the cost and placement of funds. The textbook distinguishes bank-rate lending without collateral from repo lending against securities under a repurchase agreement. Raising lending rates tends to raise borrowing cost and reduce credit demand; lowering them works in the opposite direction. RBI sales of securities absorb liquidity and reduce credit creation; RBI purchases release liquidity and increase it. These are textbook mechanisms, not a claim about today’s policy rates.',source='IMG_0557')
L(6,'Reserve ratios and qualitative instruments','CRR is the percentage of deposits banks must keep with the RBI. SLR requires banks to maintain prescribed liquid assets such as cash, gold and approved securities. Higher reserve requirements constrain lending; lower requirements release lending capacity. Margin requirement is the difference between collateral value and loan value. Higher margins reduce permitted loans. Rationing fixes credit quotas, while moral suasion uses persuasion and pressure to influence bank lending. Quantitative instruments affect overall credit; qualitative instruments target its allocation.',source='IMG_0558')
L(6,'Ability Zone: when monetary tools succeed','Repo-rate policy depends on banks’ reliance on RBI loans, responsiveness of credit demand, the wider structure of interest rates and availability of non-bank funds. Open market operations require a functioning securities market and an effective link between reserve changes and lending. Explaining a policy requires the transmission chain: policy change → bank funds or borrowing cost → credit → expenditure → aggregate demand.',source='IMG_0559',kind='Ability Zone')
L(7,'Aggregate demand and aggregate supply','Aggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure. In a two-sector economy AD = C + I; a closed economy with government adds G; an open economy adds X − M. Aggregate supply refers to production planned by producers. In the textbook’s short-run model with constant prices and spare capacity, AS is identified with income/output and represented by the 45° line.','AD = C + I + G + (X − M)\nAS = Y = C + S',source='IMG_0562')
L(7,'Consumption function: autonomous and induced consumption','Consumption function is the functional relationship between consumption and income. Autonomous consumption is consumption when income is zero; induced consumption is the part that varies with income. In C = C̄ + bY, the vertical intercept is C̄ and the slope b is MPC. Consumption rises with income, but normally by less than the rise in income. A change in income moves along a given function; a change in autonomous consumption shifts it.','C = C̄ + bY\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY\nInduced consumption = bY',source='IMG_0564')
L(7,'APC and MPC: totals versus increments','Average propensity to consume is total consumption divided by total income; marginal propensity to consume is additional consumption divided by additional income. APC may exceed one when consumption exceeds income. MPC is the slope of the consumption curve; APC is not. With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC falls as income rises. At zero income APC is undefined.','APC = C ÷ Y\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY',source='IMG_0566')
L(7,'Saving function and the break-even point','Saving is income not consumed. Derive S by subtracting the entire consumption function from Y. The saving function has a negative intercept equal to autonomous consumption and a slope equal to MPS. At break-even income C = Y and S = 0: APC is one and APS is zero. Below it saving is negative; above it saving is positive. Break-even is not necessarily equilibrium, because equilibrium also depends on investment.','S = Y − C = −C̄ + (1 − b)Y\nBreak-even income = C̄ ÷ (1 − b)',source='IMG_0567')
L(7,'APS, MPS and the two identities','Average propensity to save is S/Y; marginal propensity to save is ΔS/ΔY. APS can be negative when households dissave. MPS is the slope of the saving function. Since income is split between consumption and saving, APC + APS = 1 and MPC + MPS = 1. Do not confuse ΔS (change in saving) with AS (aggregate supply): similar letters represent different concepts.','APS = S ÷ Y; MPS = ΔS ÷ ΔY\nAPC + APS = 1; MPC + MPS = 1',source='IMG_0568')
L(7,'Investment and deriving one function from the other','In the simple model autonomous investment is independent of income, so its curve is horizontal. Induced investment responds to changes in income or activity. The expected profitability of investment and rate of interest influence investment decisions. To obtain C from S, use C = Y − S; to obtain S from C, use S = Y − C. The two slopes add to one and their vertical intercepts have equal magnitudes with opposite signs.',source='IMG_0572')
L(8,'Short run and equilibrium output','The textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output. Equilibrium output is the level where intended production equals intended expenditure. Producers have no unplanned change in inventories and no incentive to change output. In the two-sector economy Y = C + I and Y = C + S, so the same equilibrium condition can be written S = I.','Y = AD = C + I\nC + S = C + I ⇒ S = I\nY* = (C̄ + I) ÷ (1 − MPC)',source='IMG_0573')
L(8,'How inventories restore equilibrium','When AD is below AS, goods remain unsold and actual inventories exceed desired inventories. Producers cut output and employment until planned saving equals planned investment. When AD exceeds AS, inventories fall unexpectedly and producers expand output, provided spare capacity exists. The direction is easy to reverse by mistake: excess supply causes inventory accumulation; excess demand causes inventory depletion.',source='IMG_0574')
L(8,'Ex-ante versus ex-post saving and investment','Ex-ante means planned; ex-post means realised. Planned saving and planned investment need not be equal away from equilibrium. Actual saving and actual investment are equal by accounting identity because realised investment includes unintended inventory changes. Equilibrium requires equality of planned S and planned I, not merely the ex-post identity.',source='IMG_0575',kind='Purple-box concept')
L(8,'Effective demand and underemployment equilibrium','Effective demand is aggregate demand at the equilibrium level of income/output. Equilibrium can occur below full employment: firms’ production plans can match spending plans while workers and productive capacity remain idle. An increase in autonomous expenditure shifts AD upward and raises equilibrium output in the model with spare capacity; a fall lowers it.',source='IMG_0576')
L(8,'Investment multiplier: size and derivation','Investment multiplier is the ratio of change in income to change in investment. Starting with ΔY = ΔC + ΔI and ΔC = MPC × ΔY gives ΔY(1 − MPC) = ΔI. Higher MPC means more income is respent, so the multiplier is larger. Higher MPS means more income leaks into saving, so the multiplier is smaller. With MPC zero, k = 1; as MPC approaches one, k becomes very large.','k = ΔY ÷ ΔI = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPS\nΔY = k × ΔI',source='IMG_0579')
L(8,'Multiplier mechanism, assumptions and reverse operation','An investment injection is someone’s income. They consume a fraction, which becomes someone else’s income; successive rounds generate ΔI, MPC × ΔI, MPC² × ΔI and so on. Saving reduces each next round. The simple multiplier assumes constant MPC, available spare capacity, constant prices, and no additional leakages such as imports or taxes. A fall in investment works in reverse, producing a multiplied fall in income. The final increase in saving equals the initial increase in investment.',source='IMG_0580')
L(9,'Full employment and unemployment','Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work. Voluntary unemployment reflects unwillingness to work at the existing wage; involuntary unemployment means willing workers cannot find jobs. The textbook also distinguishes natural unemployment: frictional unemployment during job transitions and structural unemployment caused by changing industries, skills or production techniques. Underemployment equilibrium has AD = AS with unutilised capacity.',source='IMG_0583')
L(9,'Deficient demand and deflationary gap','Deficient demand occurs when aggregate demand is less than the aggregate supply corresponding to full employment. The deflationary gap is the shortfall of AD from the level required to maintain full employment; measure it vertically at full-employment output. It reduces output and employment and creates involuntary unemployment. The gap in expenditure is not the same as the shortfall in equilibrium income: the multiplier links the two.','Deflationary gap = Yf − AD at Yf\nRequired increase in autonomous expenditure = (Yf − Y*) ÷ k',source='IMG_0584')
L(9,'Excess demand and inflationary gap','Excess demand occurs when AD exceeds the demand required to sustain full employment. The inflationary gap is the excess of AD over full-employment AS, measured at Yf. Once resources are fully employed, real output cannot expand in the simple model; excess spending raises the general price level. A calculated equilibrium above Yf is a signal of inflationary pressure, not evidence that the economy can actually produce beyond its capacity.','Inflationary gap = AD at Yf − Yf',source='IMG_0585')
L(9,'Fiscal measures: expenditure, taxation and borrowing','To correct deficient demand, raise government expenditure, reduce taxes, reduce public borrowing and increase borrowing from the central bank, following the textbook framework. These measures raise spending power or public expenditure. For excess demand, reverse the direction. Explain the chain: lower taxes → higher disposable income → more consumption → higher AD. Borrowing from the public can withdraw purchasing power; borrowing from the central bank can expand it.',source='IMG_0586')
L(9,'Monetary measures: cost and availability of credit','For deficient demand, lower bank/repo/reverse-repo rates, lower CRR and SLR, buy securities, lower margins, relax credit rationing and encourage lending. For excess demand, raise rates and reserve requirements, sell securities, raise margins and restrict lending. Monetary policy is the central bank’s policy; fiscal policy is the government’s revenue and expenditure policy. Always connect the instrument to credit and then to aggregate demand.',source='IMG_0592')
L(10,'Government budget and its objectives','Government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy. Objectives include GDP growth, allocation of resources, redistribution of income and wealth, economic stability, management of public enterprises and employment generation. Public infrastructure can encourage private investment; progressive taxation and social spending can reduce inequality. Fiscal discipline means managing public finances responsibly to avoid unsustainable deficits and debt.',source='IMG_0598')
L(10,'Revenue receipts versus capital receipts','Revenue receipts neither create a liability nor reduce assets. They include tax revenue and non-tax revenue such as fees, fines, interest, profits/dividends and grants. Capital receipts create a liability or reduce assets: borrowing creates liabilities, while loan recovery and disinvestment reduce assets. Do not classify a receipt by size or regularity alone; use the liability/asset test.',source='IMG_0601')
L(10,'Direct taxes, indirect taxes and non-tax revenue','Direct taxes have impact and incidence on the same person, such as income tax and corporation tax. Indirect taxes allow the burden to be shifted, such as GST. A fee is a payment for a specific government service; a fine penalises an offence. A tax is compulsory and does not promise a direct equivalent service. Progressive taxes take a larger proportion as income rises; proportional taxes keep the same proportion; regressive burdens fall relatively more heavily on lower incomes.',source='IMG_0600')
L(10,'Revenue expenditure versus capital expenditure','Revenue expenditure neither creates assets nor reduces liabilities. Salaries, pensions, subsidies and interest payments are examples. Capital expenditure creates assets or reduces liabilities: construction of a highway, purchase of equipment, lending by government and repayment of loan principal. Repayment of principal is capital expenditure; interest is revenue expenditure. Grants to states remain revenue expenditure for the granting government even if the recipient uses them to build assets, following the textbook convention.',source='IMG_0602')
L(10,'Revenue deficit, fiscal deficit and primary deficit','Revenue deficit is the excess of revenue expenditure over revenue receipts. Fiscal deficit is total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings; it measures borrowing requirements. Primary deficit deducts interest payments from fiscal deficit and indicates the current borrowing requirement excluding past-debt interest. A zero revenue deficit does not guarantee zero fiscal deficit. A zero primary deficit means borrowing covers interest payments.','Revenue deficit = Revenue expenditure − Revenue receipts\nFiscal deficit = Total expenditure − Revenue receipts − Non-debt capital receipts\nPrimary deficit = Fiscal deficit − Interest payments',source='IMG_0604')
L(10,'Implications, balanced and unbalanced budgets','A revenue deficit indicates government dissaving: borrowing may finance current consumption rather than asset creation. Fiscal deficits may raise debt and interest burdens and crowd out private borrowing; financing expenditure can also support demand and productive capacity, depending on its use. A balanced budget has receipts equal to expenditure; a surplus budget has receipts above expenditure and can restrain inflation; a deficit budget can support demand in recession. Consider both the size and composition of expenditure.',source='IMG_0606')
L(10,'Ability Zone: public, private, common and club goods','Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence. Private goods are rival and excludable. Common goods are rival but non-excludable, such as fish in an open pond. Club goods are excludable but non-rival until congestion, such as a subscription service. Rivalry means one person’s use reduces availability to others; excludability means users can be denied access. Public expenditure supports infrastructure, redistribution, stability and an investment-friendly environment.',source='IMG_0607',kind='Ability Zone')
L(11,'Foreign exchange and the quotation convention','Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency. Foreign exchange rate is the price paid in domestic currency for a unit of foreign currency. Throughout these lessons, quote the rate as ₹ per US dollar. A rise from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ means the rupee buys fewer dollars and has depreciated; a fall to ₹76/$ means rupee appreciation. Always identify which currency is in the numerator before describing a movement.',source='IMG_0610')
L(11,'Demand, supply and flexible exchange-rate determination','Flexible exchange rate is determined by demand and supply in the foreign exchange market. Demand for foreign exchange comes from imports, overseas investment, foreign travel, loan repayment, income payments, grants and speculation. Supply comes from exports, foreign investment, inward loans, tourism receipts, income receipts, grants and remittances. With ₹/$ on the vertical axis, demand slopes downward and supply upward; their intersection is the equilibrium rate. A higher exchange rate makes foreign goods costlier in rupees and normally reduces import-related foreign-exchange demand.',source='IMG_0611')
L(11,'Movements versus shifts, appreciation versus depreciation','A change in the exchange rate causes movement along a given demand or supply curve. A change in imports, investment preferences, income or foreign demand shifts a curve. Greater demand for dollars shifts demand right, raising ₹/$ and depreciating the rupee. Greater supply of dollars shifts supply right, lowering ₹/$ and appreciating the rupee. Depreciation can make exports cheaper for foreigners and imports costlier for residents; actual quantity responses depend on demand conditions.',source='IMG_0613')
L(11,'Fixed rate, devaluation and revaluation','Fixed exchange rate is set and maintained by the monetary authority. An official rise in ₹/$ is devaluation of the rupee; an official fall is revaluation. Depreciation/appreciation describe market-driven changes under a flexible system. To defend a fixed rate when demand for dollars exceeds supply, the authority sells foreign currency from reserves; when dollar supply exceeds demand it buys foreign currency. An overvalued domestic currency tends to create excess demand for foreign currency at the official rate.',source='IMG_0614')
L(11,'Managed floating and merits/demerits','Managed floating combines market determination with occasional central-bank intervention to limit undesirable movements; the textbook also calls it dirty floating. Fixed rates reduce exchange uncertainty and can support trade but require reserves and may misallocate resources. Flexible rates need less reserve defence and adjust through markets but create uncertainty and exposure to external shocks. The original comparison covers the full list of merits and demerits.',source='IMG_0616')
L(11,'Foreign exchange market: transfer, credit and hedging','The foreign exchange market is the market for national currencies. The transfer function moves purchasing power between countries. The credit function supports trade through financing arrangements. Hedging reduces exchange-rate risk. Spot transactions exchange currency for current delivery; forward transactions agree today on a rate for delivery at a future date. A forward contract fixes an exchange rate; it does not guarantee that the future spot rate will equal that rate.',source='IMG_0618')
L(12,'Balance of payments: account and recording rule','Balance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year. It records goods, services, income, transfers and asset-related transactions between residents and non-residents. Foreign-exchange receipts are credit items; payments are debit items. The textbook uses current, capital and official-reserves accounts. Keep its school-level account terminology when answering.',source='IMG_0620')
L(12,'Current account, trade balance and invisibles','Current account records exports and imports of goods and services, factor income and current transfers. Goods are visible trade or merchandise; services, factor incomes and transfers are invisibles. Balance of trade covers goods only. A gift from abroad is a current transfer; wages for a service are factor income. The supplied purple box treats direct tourist purchases as invisible trade: preserve that convention for this textbook.','Trade balance = Goods exports − Goods imports\nCurrent-account balance = Trade balance + Net services + Net factor income + Net current transfers',source='IMG_0621')
L(12,'Capital account and asset/liability changes','Capital account records transactions that change a country’s assets or liabilities in relation to the rest of the world. The textbook includes borrowing, foreign investment, NRI deposits, banking capital and short-term debt. Inward FDI or an external loan is a credit; an outward investment or repayment of foreign loan principal is a debit. Interest on a foreign loan belongs in the current account; the principal belongs in the capital account. FDI involves a lasting productive interest/control; portfolio investment purchases financial securities.',source='IMG_0622')
L(12,'Official reserves, deficit and surplus','If autonomous foreign-exchange payments exceed autonomous receipts, there is a BoP deficit, financed by accommodating transactions such as a drawdown of reserves or official borrowing. A surplus can increase official reserves. A decrease in reserve assets finances a shortfall; an increase uses foreign exchange. Follow the sign convention explicitly stated in a numerical instead of confusing a positive stock increase with a credit entry.',source='IMG_0624')
L(12,'Autonomous versus accommodating transactions','Autonomous transactions are undertaken for independent economic motives such as profit, rather than to correct a BoP imbalance; they are above-the-line items. Accommodating transactions are undertaken to finance or correct the imbalance and are below the line. A foreign loan may be autonomous when raised commercially or accommodating when arranged specifically to finance a deficit. Classification depends on purpose, not merely the type of transaction.',source='IMG_0625',kind='Purple-box concept')
L(12,'Accounting balance versus operational balance','BoP balances in the accounting sense because every transaction has a counterpart and financing entries close the accounts. This does not mean autonomous receipts equal autonomous payments or that the economy has no external problem. A current-account deficit financed by foreign borrowing is consistent with accounting balance and increasing indebtedness. Read the Ability Zone worked table to see a current-account deficit offset by capital inflows.',source='IMG_0627',kind='Ability Zone')
L(12,'Causes and significance of disequilibrium','Economic causes include large development imports, business cycles, domestic inflation, import substitution and changes in trading partners’ costs. Political instability can discourage foreign investment; policy changes can increase imports. Social causes include changes in tastes and cross-border preferences. BoP data show external strengths, weaknesses, debt and reserve pressures and help formulate policy. A trade deficit alone does not tell you the whole BoP position.',source='IMG_0627')
